      "title": "Time limit on certain defenses",
      "narration": "After the policy has been in force for two years, the insurer may no longer use misstatements in the application, other than fraudulent ones, to void the policy or deny a claim. It also may not deny a claim after those two years by calling a condition pre-existing unless the policy excluded it by name. This is the health insurance counterpart to the life insurance incontestability clause. Rosa’s innocent error about a past prescription cannot be raised against her in year three. Deliberate fraud remains contestable, and some policies use the stricter incontestable wording that drops even the fraud exception.",
